{"hash":"54ebac404fe6aab5ce64f41a454af3831a29c363","data":{"metadata":{"siteName":"eliakorkmazblog","siteDescription":"Technology blog of myself; I write generally what I learned"},"post":{"id":"dabf06231d2d7b6e90f041f45e2c929c","title":"Integrate Firebase Manually into iOS App","content":"<p>Let's assume we want to build an app for iOS / macOS or tvOS. We only want to use Swift Package Manager as Package Manager and we know we have to use Firebase. Maybe you have similar reasons, you don't want to change project structure with using <code class=\"language-text\">Cocoapods</code> and maybe don't want to use <code class=\"language-text\">Carthage</code> etc. I encounter with this situation and solve with a little bit struggling way because even Google's documentation is not complete in this integration ways.</p>\n<p>First of all, I highly recommend you to read <code class=\"language-text\">Firebase</code> documentation about manual integration on here <a href=\"https://firebase.google.com/docs/ios/setup#frameworks\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow noopener noreferrer\">Firebase Integration</a>. Let's start making some manual integrations 🙂</p>\n<p>As you read in documentation, you have to download <code class=\"language-text\">Firebase SDK</code> files from Google. It took approximately ~200MB. You have to see a directory like below;</p>\n<p><img class=\"g-image g-image--lazy g-image--loading\" src=\"data:image/svg+xml,%3csvg fill='none' viewBox='0 0 640 888' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' xmlns:xlink='http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink'%3e%3cdefs%3e%3cfilter id='__svg-blur-b7df0a4dfdbb1fc171f245740cad1d15'%3e%3cfeGaussianBlur in='SourceGraphic' stdDeviation='40'/%3e%3c/filter%3e%3c/defs%3e%3cimage x='0' y='0' filter='url(%23__svg-blur-b7df0a4dfdbb1fc171f245740cad1d15)' width='640' height='888' xlink:href='data:image/png%3bbase64%2ciVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAEAAAABZCAYAAACOsCKNAAAACXBIWXMAABYlAAAWJQFJUiTwAAAZUUlEQVR42uWcaWydWXnH/R1pIJv36%2b1636/35XqJl3iNl8T2xLsnsWPHdmI7